# Campus Networking Project


![alt text]({9A2D455F-5A35-4634-8ADD-EA3AEC64B14B}.png)


A complete enterprise-grade network infrastructure design and prototype for a multi-campus university using **Cisco Packet Tracer**. The project implements a secure, scalable, and hierarchical network model providing end-to-end connectivity across multiple departments and geographical locations.

## 📌 Project Overview

University requires a robust network infrastructure to support two campuses situated 20 miles apart: the **Main Campus** and the **Branch Campus**. The network is designed using a three-layer hierarchical architecture (Core, Distribution, and Access) to separate distinct faculties, manage traffic efficiently, and secure campus assets.

### Key Capabilities
*   **Segmented Architecture:** 10 distinct departments isolated into dedicated Virtual Local Area Networks (VLANs).
*   **Dynamic IP Allocation:** Automated client configuration using router-based DHCP pools.
*   **Inter-VLAN Routing:** Router-on-a-Stick sub-interface configuration allowing controlled cross-department communication.
*   **Hybrid Routing Domain:** Dynamic routing inside the campus intranet running **RIPv2**, with static routing pointing out to external cloud assets.

---

## 🛠️ Technologies & Protocols Used

*   **Network Simulation:** Cisco Packet Tracer (v8.x preferred)
*   **Architecture Model:** Cisco Three-Layer Hierarchical Model (Core, Distribution, Access)
*   **Routing Protocols:**
    *   **RIPv2 (Routing Information Protocol v2):** Classless dynamic routing protocol utilized across all internal campus networks.
    *   **Static Routing:** Managed links pointing to external cloud environments.
*   **Switching Protocols:**
    *   **IEEE 802.1Q:** Frame tagging protocol used to manage trunking between Multi-Layer and Access switches.
    *   **Inter-VLAN Routing:** Configured through hardware sub-interfaces ("Router-on-a-Stick").
*   **Infrastructure Services:**
    *   **DHCP Server:** Built directly into Cisco iOS routers to hand out dynamic IPs, default gateways, and DNS records dynamically.

